   β² = α h + γ ,  deg_y γ < deg_y h = 5 ,
```
and then **the requirement that the `y^{-1}`-power series cancel** (because
`g` is a polynomial) produces the exact identities
`β³ = (3γ+a)h² + εh + δ` with `deg_y ε, δ < 4` and
`(1/2)ε = b f^{4/10} + c(x³+d*)y + O(y^{-1})`; combined with
"*the inverse transformation implies that there is a minor disc, thus in the
major disc `D_2` there are precisely three subdiscs which contain 2, 2, 6 of
roots `f`*" and "*the datum `δ_1 = 1/2` implies that the polynomial* `h` …".
On `(16,12; 13; 3; X)` (p.208) the same shape with
`ḡ = h⁴+α_1h³+α_2h²+α_3h+α_4`, `f̄ = h³+β_2h+β_3`, `deg_y h = 4`,
`deg_y α_i, β_i ≤ 3`, `ord α_i(σ) ≥ i(−1/4)`, `ord h(σ) ≥ −1/4`.

Note `deg_y h = 4 = d_2'` on `(16,12)` and `deg_y h = 5 = d_2'` on
